  "title": "pos prompt",
  "properties": {
   "cnr_id": "comfy-core",
   "ver": "0.10.0",
   "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"
  },
  "widgets_values": [
   ""
  ],
  "widgets_values_named": {
   "value": "Black and white documentary photograph, 1963, a records office, seen from high above and behind. A man sits cross-legged on the floor with his back fully to the camera, head bowed, no face visible, small and low, screen left in the frame: white shirt with sleeves rolled, dark trousers. He holds a single slim new folder up in both hands in front of him, clean and pale and crisp against all the worn paper around him, reading it. Stacked in a dense bank filling the whole upper part of the picture behind him are dozens of plain fibreboard document boxes, lids off and set aside, piled two and three high, each with a handwritten label. Covering the floor all around him in the lower part of the picture are thick uneven stacks of paper, string-tied bundles and manila folders pulled out of those boxes, some slumped and leaning, some fanned open, none of them squared up or aligned, the typing on them far too small to read. In the near foreground one box stands open and tipped toward the camera and it is completely empty. The boxes crowd more heavily on the left than the right. A window off to the side throws flat even daylight across the floor. Even overall illumination, open shadows, full detail in the shade, nothing falling to black. Wide lens, high overhead angle looking down over his shoulder, deep focus, everything sharp front to back, no blur anywhere. Working, not posed. Clean neutral monochrome, full tonal range, no film grain, no dust, no border, no logos, no captions, no printed branding on the boxes, no sticky notes, no plastic."
  },
  "color": "#232",
  "bgcolor": "#353"
 },
